</math> kilograms traveling at a speed of <math alttext="v"><mi>v</mi>
</math> meters per second is given by the function <math alttext="upper K"><mi>K</mi>
</math>, where <math alttext="upper K left parenthesis v right parenthesis equals nine halves v squared"><mi>K</mi><mfenced><mi>v</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. It follows that in the equation <math alttext="upper K left parenthesis 34 right parenthesis equals 5,202"><mi>K</mi><mfenced><mn>34</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>5,202</mn></math>, <math alttext="34"><mn>34</mn>
</math> is the value of <math alttext="v"><mi>v</mi>
</math>, or the speed of the object, in meters per second, and <math alttext="5,202"><mn>5,202</mn>
</math> is the kinetic energy, in joules, of the object at that speed. Therefore, the best interpretation of <math alttext="upper K left parenthesis 34 right parenthesis equals 5,202"><mi>K</mi><mfenced><mn>34</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>5,202</mn></math> in this context is the object traveling at <math alttext="34"><mn>34</mn>
</math> meters per second has a kinetic energy of <math alttext="5,202"><mn>5,202</mn>
</math> joules.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is incorrect. The object traveling at <math alttext="340"><mn>340</mn>
</math> meters per second has a kinetic energy of <math alttext="520,200"><mn>520,200</mn>
</math> joules.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. The object traveling at <math alttext="5,202"><mn>5,202</mn>
</math> meters per second has a kinetic energy of <math alttext="121,773,618"><mn>121,773,618</mn>
</math> joules.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. The object traveling at <math alttext="23,409"><mn>23,409</mn>
